<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Nueva Vizcaya</h1><p class="st-meta">1562 CE – 1821 CE · capital Durango</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The vast northern Spanish province of New Spain, covering today's Durango and Chihuahua, built on silver mining and frontier warfare.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-ibarra">Francisco de Ibarra</a>, a Basque explorer funded by his uncle, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> mining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a>, led expeditions north from 1554 and became governor of the new province in 1562. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durango-city">Durango</a> in 1563 as its capital and named the province after his home region, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The province's wealth came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mines at Santa Bárbara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hidalgo-del-parral">Parral</a>, from 1631, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chihuahua-city">Chihuahua</a>. Missions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> pushed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-madre-occidental">Sierra Madre</a>, provoking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tepehuan-revolt">Tepehuán revolt</a> of 1616 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raramuri">Tarahumara</a> uprisings. Presidios guarded routes against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> and other raiders, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-real-de-tierra-adentro">Camino Real</a> linked it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/santa-fe-de-nuevo-mexico">Santa Fe de Nuevo Mexico</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 1776 Nueva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Vizcaya</a> belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces">Provincias Internas</a>, and in 1786 it became an intendancy. After independence it was divided in 1824 into the Mexican states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durango-city">Durango</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chihuahua-city">Chihuahua</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nueva-vizcaya.jpg" alt="Nueva Vizcaya" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: Shubert Ciencia from Nueva Ecija, Philippines · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sta._Fe,_Nueva_Vizcaya_(4458904289).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1562 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Durango</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco de Ibarra</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>New Biscay, Province of Nueva Vizcaya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1692&amp;focus=nueva-vizcaya" aria-label="Nueva Vizcaya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1896 1260 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nueva Vizcaya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2216 1516L2321 1717M2216 1516L2142 1327"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0M2142 1327h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2216 1516h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2216 1516h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Durango. <a href="/atlas?year=1692&amp;focus=nueva-vizcaya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

